tion": "324 ⟦1⟧ still ⟦2⟧. 251. ⟦3⟧ (Conington);"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-2-mentioned1", "translation": "son"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-comment1", "translation": "326 ⟦1⟧ according to the Attic ⟦2⟧, Meier-Schömann-Lipsius Att. Proc. 658. ⟦3⟧ is in Homer an epithet of the hare or stands for the animal itself, P 676, as in A. ⟦4⟧ Ag. 137 (⟦5⟧), which (⟦6⟧) M also had here at first; is this then the meaning, or \"fugitive\", after ⟦7⟧ 252? ⟦8⟧. a due expiatory offering to the mother for the murder; it is not absolutely necessary to assume a transposition in relation to the associated noun, since ⟦9⟧. properly belongs to ⟦10⟧ (⟦11⟧ ⟦12⟧ and other places, Wecklein on 292)."}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned1", "translation": "taking away"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned2", "translation": "removal to freedom"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned3", "translation": "cowering"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned4", "translation": "cowering"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned5", "translation": "a wretched cowering creature for those sacrificing"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned6", "translation": "cowering creature"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned7", "translation": "having cowered down"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned8", "translation": "a mother's expiatory offering"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned9", "translation": "mother"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned10", "translation": "of murder"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-3-mentioned11", "translation": "the weight of the mother's name"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-4-comment1", "translation": "⟦1⟧ cf. 127, effective, intended for this purpose. Refrain 329 ff."}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-4-mentioned1", "translation": "lord"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-5-comment1", "translation": "⟦1⟧ cf. 304 f., thus already as good as actually sacrificed; to this sacrifice this song."}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-5-mentioned1", "translation": "to the one sacrificed"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-comment1", "translation": "⟦1⟧ ⟦2⟧ (⟦3⟧ ⟦4⟧), ⟦5⟧ cf. ⟦6⟧ and ⟦7⟧. There is no reason in form or meaning to doubt ⟦8⟧, cf. ⟦9⟧, ⟦10⟧ ⟦11⟧; ⟦12⟧ stands A. Frg. 121. But neither do the rhythms: ~, -, -, - ⟦13⟧; still more similar, -,, -, -, - ⟦14⟧ (Paley). With the conjecture written in M (⟦15⟧) ⟦16⟧ nothing can be done; of the newer conjectures (-⟦17⟧, -⟦18⟧, -⟦19⟧, -⟦20⟧) none is any more commendable."}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned1", "translation": "madness"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned2", "translation": "maddened"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned3", "translation": "derangement"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned4", "translation": "deranged"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned5", "translation": "to be deranged"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned6", "translation": "mind-destroying"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned7", "translation": "mind-harming"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned8", "translation": "mind-stricken madnesses"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned9", "translation": "destruction"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned10", "translation": "I think"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned11", "translation": "mind-destroying"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned12", "translation": "wandering"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned13", "translation": "harming"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned14", "translation": "raging"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-6-mentioned15", "translation": "biting"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-7-comment1", "translation": "⟦1⟧ is evidently the more terrible, because less transparent name (still 511 chorus, 951 Athena); at the introduction 417 they call themselves ⟦2⟧."}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-7-mentioned1", "translation": "of the Erinyes"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-7-mentioned2", "translation": "Curses"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-8-comment1", "translation": "⟦1⟧: thus (as is natural) this song was not accompanied by Apollo's cithara (but only by the shrill and exciting flute). Cf. (Stanley) ⟦2⟧ ⟦3⟧."}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-8-mentioned1", "translation": "without lyre"}, {"id": "mvp-comment-137-8-quote1", "translation": "but without the lyre, nevertheless the self-taught spirit within sings a dirge of the Erinys"}]}